   "name": "Renewable energy sources and their uses"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Renewable energy sources are replaced by nature and will not run out: the sun (solar energy), wind, flowing water (hydroelectricity), biomass (wood, crop waste, animal dung) and the heat of the Earth (geothermal).\nNon-renewable sources (petrol, diesel, coal, natural gas) took millions of years to form and will run out; burning them pollutes the air.\nUses: solar panels for light and phones, solar water heaters, hydroelectric dams (Cameroon gets much of its electricity from dams such as Edéa, Song Loulou and Lom Pangar), windmills for pumping water, biogas from dung for cooking.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Is firewood renewable? Is diesel?",
  "steps": [
   "Firewood comes from trees, which can grow again if we replant them: renewable (biomass).",
   "Diesel comes from petroleum, formed over millions of years: non-renewable."
  ],
  "answer": "Firewood: renewable if trees are replanted; diesel: non-renewable.",
  "checks": []
 },
